Subject: [PATCH 11147/11362] [outer-loop] Announce a self-unseal instead of
 honouring it silently (SCENARIO-HARNESS-INTEGRITY-1-B)

Second of the three triaged audit findings, and the oldest at seven days.

A path named in any active declaration's unsealed list is unsealed for that
commit. That bypass is deliberate for ordinary harness files. Naming THIS file
permits an uncommitted edit to the check that decides whether edits may land.

My first fix refused the self-unseal outright, and the guard proved that wrong
by refusing the commit carrying it. This file is sealed by the standing
declaration, so a refusal makes it uneditable: the commit that would move the
HEAD baseline is the one being refused, and the refusal text advertises an
--unseal that would no longer work. A guard that cannot be repaired is not
safer. Recorded rather than quietly replaced, because the failure is the
instructive part.

The finding is SILENT non-firing, and the cure for silence is noise rather than
a wall. The self-unseal now proceeds and every run honouring it prints a NOTE
naming the file and saying the check is disarmed against its own edit. Ordinary
unseals stay quiet, so the line that matters is not buried; a mutation covers
that distinction.

7 tests, 3/3 mutations RED. This commit landed through the very path it
governs: a declared scope naming the file, with the new NOTE firing.

